<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cudjoe Lewis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1841–<a href="/years/1935">1935</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Oluale Kossola, Cudjo Lewis, Kossola</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cudjoe">Cudjoe</a> Lewis, born Oluale Kossola in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, was one of the last survivors of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. According to the historian Sylviane Diouf a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba</a> from the Banté region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin</a>, he was captured in 1860 in a dry-season raid by the female warriors of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glele">Glele</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-dahomey">Dahomey</a>, sold at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ouidah">Ouidah</a> and carried to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alabama">Alabama</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clotilda-slave-ship">Clotilda</a>. He worked as a de facto slave of the Meaher family until the end of the Civil War, then helped found the community at Magazine Point north of Mobile, now the Africatown Historic District. In old age he told the history of the Clotilda captives to visitors, among them Emma Langdon <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoffmann-la-roche">Roche</a> and Zora Neale Hurston.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cudjoe-lewis.jpg" alt="Cudjoe Lewis" width="330" height="419" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Emma Langdon Roche · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cudjoe_Lewis_photo_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
